**Problem.** Find all $f: \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R}^+$ with $f(x + f(y+xy)) = (y+1)f(x+1) - 1$ for all $x,y \in \mathbb{R}^+$.

---

**Step 1. Reformulation.** Since $y + xy = y(1+x)$, substituting $u = y(1+x)$ (so $y = u/(1+x) > 0$ for any $u > 0$) gives, for all $x, u > 0$:
$$f\bigl(x + f(u)\bigr) = \frac{u+1+x}{1+x}\,f(x+1) - 1. \tag{I}$$

**Step 2. Injectivity.** If $f(u_1)=f(u_2)$, then the LHS of (I) coincides for $u_1,u_2$, while the RHS equals $\frac{f(x+1)}{1+x}(u_i+1+x)-1$. Since $\frac{f(x+1)}{1+x}>0$, equality forces $u_1=u_2$. So $f$ is **injective**.

**Step 3. The range contains an interval.** For fixed $x>0$, the RHS of (I) is $\frac{f(x+1)}{1+x}(u+1+x)-1$, which as $u$ ranges over $(0,\infty)$ takes exactly all values in $\bigl(f(x+1)-1,\,\infty\bigr)$. Hence
$$\operatorname{Range}(f) \supseteq \bigl(f(x+1)-1,\,\infty\bigr) \quad\text{for every } x>0.$$
Let $m := \inf_{t>1} f(t) - 1$. Then $\operatorname{Range}(f) \supseteq (m,\infty)$. Since the range is contained in $(0,\infty)$, we have $m\ge 0$. In particular $\operatorname{Range}(f)-\operatorname{Range}(f) \supseteq (m,\infty)-(m,\infty) = \mathbb{R}$, so **every real number is a difference of two range values**.

